[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Transaction Management and Propagation in JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Transaction management is a critical aspect of enterprise application development. It ensures data consistency and integrity by grouping a series of database operations into a single atomic unit of work. In Java Persistence API (JPA) and Spring, understanding transaction boundaries and propagation behaviors is essential for building robust enterprise systems."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding JPA Transactions"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"JPA supports two types of transaction management models: Resource-local transactions and JTA (Java Transaction API) managed transactions. Resource-local transactions are typically managed directly by the persistence provider using an EntityTransaction interface, whereas JTA transactions are managed by a Java EE container or an external transaction manager."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Resource-local: Ideal for Java SE environments or simple web applications utilizing a single database."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"JTA-managed: Required for distributed transactions across multiple databases, message queues, or enterprise services."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Transaction Propagation in Spring with JPA"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"When integrating JPA with Spring, the @Transactional annotation provides declarative transaction management. Transaction propagation defines how transactions relate to each other when one transactional method calls another transactional method."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"REQUIRED (Default): Supports a current transaction; creates a new one if none exists."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"REQUIRES_NEW: Creates a new transaction, suspending the current transaction if one exists."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SUPPORTS: Supports a current transaction; executes non-transactionally if none exists."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"NOT_SUPPORTED: Executes non-transactionally, suspending the current transaction if any."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Implementing Propagation Behaviors"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example demonstrating how to configure and use different propagation levels in a Spring-managed JPA service layer."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Autowired
    private BillingService billingService;

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED)
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        // Save primary order details
        orderRepository.save(order);

        // Call billing with a separate independent transaction
        billingService.processPayment(order.getBillingDetails());
    }
}

@Service
public class BillingService {

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void processPayment(BillingDetails details) {
        // This executes in its own transaction context
        billingRepository.save(details);
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for JPA Transactions"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep transactions as short as possible to avoid connection pool exhaustion and locking issues."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Apply @Transactional at the service layer rather than the repository layer to properly encapsulate business logic."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use readOnly = true on query-heavy methods to allow database-level optimizations."}]
